'''Jamelle Antoine Bouie''' ({{IPAc-en|dZ|@|'|m|E|l|_|'|b|u:|.|i:}}; born 1987)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.smcvt.edu/about-smc/news/2026/march/the-line-we-must-not-cross/|title=The Line We Must Not Cross: Applying Catholic Social Teaching to Contemporary Issues|first=David|last=Theroux|date=March 25, 2026}}</ref> is an American columnist for ''[[The New York Times]]''. He was formerly chief political correspondent for ''[[Slate (magazine)|Slate]]''.

== Early and personal life ==
Bouie was born and raised in [[Virginia Beach, Virginia]]. He graduated from [[Floyd E. Kellam High School]] in 2005.<ref>{{cite tweet|last=Bouie|first=Jamelle|user=jbouie|number=1189897894314356739|date=October 31, 2019|title=officially the most famous person from my high school|access-date=August 13, 2020|archive-date=October 31, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191031135822/https://twitter.com/jbouie/status/1189897894314356739|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2009, he graduated from the [[University of Virginia]]<ref name="UVA">{{cite book |url=https://majorevents.virginia.edu/sites/majorevents2017.virginia.edu/files/Finals_Program_2009.pdf |title=One Hundred and Eightieth Final Exercises |date=May 17, 2009 |page=24 |publisher=University of Virginia |access-date=February 12, 2021}}</ref> with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree, with majors in political and social thought and government.<ref>{{cite web|title=Hoos in the News (Alumni Panel)|url=http://alumni.virginia.edu/learn/program/hoos-news-alumni-panel/|website=Lifetime Learning|publisher=University of Virginia|access-date=January 19, 2016|archive-date=October 4, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151004224431/http://alumni.virginia.edu/learn/program/hoos-news-alumni-panel/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.cjr.org/united_states_project/charlottesville-racism-journalism.php|title=Lessons on covering race and racism after Charlottesville|last=Fitzgerald|first=Brendan|date=September 21, 2017|magazine=Columbia Journalism Review|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=January 16, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190116165114/https://www.cjr.org/united_states_project/charlottesville-racism-journalism.php|url-status=live}}</ref> While there, he began blogging, which led to interest in a career in journalism.<ref name="CT">{{cite news|url = https://www.cvilletomorrow.org/articles/charlottesville-based-new-york-times-columnist-jamelle-bouie-discusses-the-past-present-and-politics/|title = Charlottesville-based New York Times columnist Jamelle Bouie discusses the past, present and politics|work = [[Charlottesville Tomorrow]]|date = June 21, 2021|access-date = August 30, 2021|last = Woods|first = Charlotte Rene}}</ref> Bouie previously lived and worked in [[Washington, D.C.]]; {{As of|2021|lc=y}}, he is based in [[Charlottesville, Virginia]].<ref name="CT" />

== Career ==

Bouie was awarded a writing fellowship for ''[[The American Prospect]]'' in 2010. He was awarded a Knobler Fellowship at the [[Nation Institute]] by ''[[The Nation]]'' in 2012.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Jamelle Bouie|url=http://www.thenation.com/authors/jamelle-bouie/|magazine=The Nation|date=March 30, 2011 |access-date=January 18, 2016|archive-date=December 20, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151220070633/http://www.thenation.com/authors/jamelle-bouie/|url-status=live}}</ref> Bouie became a staff writer for ''[[The Daily Beast]]'' in 2013,<ref>{{cite web|title=Jamelle Bouie|url=http://www.thedailybeast.com/contributors.html|website=The Daily Beast|access-date=January 19, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170504231516/http://www.thedailybeast.com/contributors.html|archive-date=May 4, 2017|url-status=dead}}</ref> writing about national politics.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Bissinger |first1=Buzz |last2=Bouie |first2=Jamelle |date=October 17, 2012 |title=Bissinger vs. Bouie: Buzz Switched to Romney, But Should He Switch Back? |url=http://nymag.com/daily/intelligencer/2012/10/bissinger-vs-bouie-on-supporting-mitt-romney.html |magazine=New York |access-date=January 19, 2016 |archive-date=December 31, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141231062945/http://nymag.com/daily/intelligencer/2012/10/bissinger-vs-bouie-on-supporting-mitt-romney.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2014, he moved to ''[[Slate (magazine)|Slate]]'' as a chief political correspondent, covering politics, policy, and race.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/media/story/2014/03/slate-hires-jamelle-bouie-from-the-daily-beast-000309/|title=Slate hires Jamelle Bouie from The Daily Beast|last=Levy|first=Nicole|date=March 24, 2014|work=Politico|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=November 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215242/https://www.politico.com/media/story/2014/03/slate-hires-jamelle-bouie-from-the-daily-beast-000309/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.adweek.com/digital/slate-expands-politics-team/|title=Slate Expands Its Politics Team|last=Grinapol|first=Corinne|date=August 18, 2015|magazine=Adweek|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=December 13, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181213151548/https://www.adweek.com/digital/slate-expands-politics-team/|url-status=live}}</ref> He joined ''[[The New York Times]]'' as a columnist in 2019.<ref name=":1">{{cite news|url=https://politi.co/2RagFUQ|last=Calderone|first=Michael|date=January 4, 2019|title=Pelosi's moment, Trump's stunt — The MSNBC primary — WaPo Style turns 50 — From Weekly Standard to The Bulwark|work=Politico|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=November 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215243/https://www.politico.com/media/newsletters/morning-media/2019/01/04/pelosis-moment-trumps-stunt-the-msnbc-primary-wapo-style-turns-50-from-weekly-standard-to-the-bulwark-001699/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.cjr.org/the_media_today/house_democrats_pelosi_oversight.php|last=Allsop|first=Jon|title=Oversight is here|date=January 4, 2019|magazine=Columbia Journalism Review|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=February 3, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190203024010/https://www.cjr.org/the_media_today/house_democrats_pelosi_oversight.php|url-status=live}}</ref>

Bouie was a contributor to ''Barack Obama and the New America: The 2012 Election and the Changing Face of Politics'',<ref>{{cite book|title=Barack Obama and the New America: The 2012 Election and the Changing Face of Politics|last1=Campbell|first1=James|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield Publishers|year=2013|isbn=978-1442222649|editor-last=Sabato|editor-first=Larry|last2=Cook|first2=Rhodes|last3=Toner|first3=Michael|last4=Owen|first4=Diana|last5=Cohn|first5=Nate|last6=Bouie|first6=Jamelle|display-authors=etal}}</ref> a 2013 book edited by political scientist [[Larry Sabato]].<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Nonfiction Book Review: Barack Obama and the New America: The 2012 Election and the Changing Face of Politics |url=http://www.publishersweekly.com/978-1-4422-2264-9 |magazine=Publishers Weekly |date=February 25, 2013 |access-date=January 19, 2016 |archive-date=November 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215326/https://www.publishersweekly.com/978-1-4422-2264-9 |url-status=live }}</ref> Bouie has been a political analyst on [[CBS News]] since 2015.<ref>{{cite news|title=Jamelle Bouie named CBS News political analyst|url=http://www.wkyt.com/home/headlines/Jamelle-Bouie-named-CBS-News-political-analyst-347645151.html|date=November 13, 2015|publisher=WKYT CBS News|access-date=January 19, 2016|archive-date=November 16, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151116173250/http://www.wkyt.com/home/headlines/Jamelle-Bouie-named-CBS-News-political-analyst-347645151.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":1" /> He frequently appears on ''[[Face the Nation]]'',<ref>{{cite news|url=http://politi.co/2AR2EPc|title=Sunday shows so far|work=Politico|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=November 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215243/https://www.politico.com/story/2018/01/12/playbook-sunday-show-lineups-january-14-337916|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2017/03/sunday-shows-236499|title=Sunday shows|work=Politico|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=February 9, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190209125655/https://www.politico.com/story/2017/03/sunday-shows-236499|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://politi.co/2GiO8Xf|title=This week's Sunday shows|work=Politico|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=November 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215256/https://www.politico.com/story/2018/03/24/this-weeks-sunday-shows-march-24-483527|url-status=live}}</ref> the network's Sunday morning show,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2017/07/22/sunday-shows-240845|title=Sunday shows|work=Politico|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=February 9, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190209125412/https://www.politico.com/story/2017/07/22/sunday-shows-240845|url-status=live}}</ref> and contributed to the network's 2016 election night coverage.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://adweek.it/2fm8crY|title=Here's How the Networks Will Cover Election Night|magazine=Adweek|date=November 7, 2016|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=November 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215256/https://www.adweek.com/tvnewser/heres-how-the-networks-will-cover-election-night/309350/|url-status=live}}</ref> Bouie writes articles focusing on history, public policy, and national politics. He also writes about entertainment, such as science fiction, comics, and film.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Marvel's Secret Wars Initiative May Have a Secret Agenda|url=http://www.slate.com/blogs/browbeat/2015/01/22/marvel_secret_wars_announcement_will_the_studio_introduce_miles_morales.html|magazine=Slate|date=January 22, 2015|access-date=January 18, 2016|last=Bouie|first=Jamelle|archive-date=December 21, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151221220200/http://www.slate.com/blogs/browbeat/2015/01/22/marvel_secret_wars_announcement_will_the_studio_introduce_miles_morales.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He has reviewed [[breakfast cereal]] for ''Serious Eats''.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Why Your Favorite Sugary Breakfast Cereal Is Suddenly Everywhere|first=Hallie|last=Lieberman|magazine=Smithsonian Magazine|url=https://www.smithsonianmag.com/arts-culture/why-your-favorite-sugary-breakfast-cereal-is-suddenly-everywhere-180979923/|date=April 18, 2022}}</ref>

Bouie has written extensively on [[racial politics]],<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.cjr.org/criticism/historians-journalists.php |last=McGuire |first=Danielle |date=April 25, 2018 |title=Historians are a great resource. Journalists, be sure to give them credit |magazine=Columbia Journalism Review |access-date=January 25, 2019 |archive-date=December 31, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181231133523/https://www.cjr.org/criticism/historians-journalists.php |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bouie |first=Jamelle |date=October 9, 2021 |title=Bill Clinton, Race and the Politics of the 1990s |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/10/09/opinion/democrat-shor-politics-bill-clinton.html |work=The New York times |access-date=July 15, 2022 |archive-date=July 10, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220710085452/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/10/09/opinion/democrat-shor-politics-bill-clinton.html |url-status=live}}</ref> including [[slavery in the United States]] and the [[American Civil War]],<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.adweek.com/digital/the-making-of-slates-inaugural-academy-series-on-slavery/|last=Grinapol|first=Corinne|date=August 14, 2015|title=The Making of Slate's Inaugural Academy Series on Slavery|magazine=Adweek|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=December 13, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181213131236/https://www.adweek.com/digital/the-making-of-slates-inaugural-academy-series-on-slavery/|url-status=live}}</ref> the [[killing of Trayvon Martin]],<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Bouie|first=Jamelle|date=April 15, 2014|title=America's Future Racial Makeup: Will Today's Hispanics Be Tomorrow's Whites?|url=http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/politics/2014/04/america_s_future_racial_makeup_will_today_s_hispanics_be_tomorrow_s_whites.html|magazine=Slate|access-date=July 15, 2022|archive-date=January 20, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160120042806/http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/politics/2014/04/america_s_future_racial_makeup_will_today_s_hispanics_be_tomorrow_s_whites.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|last=Prince|first=Richard|date=April 24, 2014|title=Hispanics Scoff at Suggestion They'll Identify as White in the Future|url=https://www.theroot.com/hispanics-scoff-at-suggestion-they-ll-identify-as-white-1790885351|magazine=The Root<!-- Journal-isms |publisher=Maynard Institute for Journalism Education|-->access-date=July 15, 2022|archive-date=August 3, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210803151126/https://www.theroot.com/hispanics-scoff-at-suggestion-they-ll-identify-as-white-1790885351|url-status=live|via=The Root}}</ref> the [[Ferguson unrest]],<ref>{{cite magazine|title=How Ferguson Changed America|url=http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/politics/2015/08/the_ferguson_anniversary_michael_brown_s_death_12_months_ago_led_to_america.html|magazine=Slate|date=August 2, 2015|access-date=January 18, 2016|last=Bouie|first= Jamelle|archive-date=January 17, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160117233317/http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/politics/2015/08/the_ferguson_anniversary_michael_brown_s_death_12_months_ago_led_to_america.html|url-status=live}}</ref> the [[Charleston church shooting]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Jon Stewart, Jamelle Bouie, And Others Weigh In On The Charleston Massacre|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/codeswitch/2015/06/19/415738452/jon-stewart-jamelle-bouie-and-others-weigh-in-on-the-charleston-massacre|last=Clarke|first=Kinsey|date=June 19, 2015|publisher=NPR|access-date=January 19, 2016|archive-date=November 6, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151106235703/http://www.npr.org/sections/codeswitch/2015/06/19/415738452/jon-stewart-jamelle-bouie-and-others-weigh-in-on-the-charleston-massacre|url-status=live}}</ref> and the [[Black Lives Matter]] movement.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Black Lives Matter Protests Matter|url=http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/politics/2015/08/black_lives_matter_and_bernie_sanders_why_the_protesters_are_so_hard_on.html|magazine=Slate|date=August 17, 2015|access-date=January 18, 2016|last=Bouie|first= Jamelle|archive-date=January 24, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160124164450/http://www.slate.com/articles/news_and_politics/politics/2015/08/black_lives_matter_and_bernie_sanders_why_the_protesters_are_so_hard_on.html|url-status=live}}</ref> His writing on racial and national politics is often quoted by other journalists.<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Views You Can Use: A Swift Reaction to Donald Sterling's Racism |url=https://www.usnews.com/opinion/articles/2014/04/30/reaction-roundup-on-nba-banning-clippers-owner-donald-sterling-for-racism |magazine=U.S. News & World Report |last=Welsh |first=Teresa |date=April 30, 2014 |access-date=January 19, 2016 |archive-date=January 9, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150109191523/http://www.usnews.com/opinion/articles/2014/04/30/reaction-roundup-on-nba-banning-clippers-owner-donald-sterling-for-racism |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Jamelle Bouie, Wrong as Usual|url=http://www.nationalreview.com/corner/378982/jamelle-bouie-wrong-usual-kevin-d-williamson|last=Williamson|first=Kevin D.|date=May 28, 2014|magazine=National Review|access-date=January 19, 2016|archive-date=March 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160308020059/http://www.nationalreview.com/corner/378982/jamelle-bouie-wrong-usual-kevin-d-williamson|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Rudy Giuliani and the 'love it or leave it' view of America|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-fix/wp/2015/02/20/rudy-giuliani-and-the-love-it-or-leave-it-view-of-america/|work=The Washington Post|date=February 20, 2015|access-date=January 19, 2016|issn=0190-8286|first=Philip|last=Bump|archive-date=September 27, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150927001448/http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-fix/wp/2015/02/20/rudy-giuliani-and-the-love-it-or-leave-it-view-of-america/|url-status=live}}</ref> Shortly after [[Donald Trump]] was elected president in 2016, Bouie wrote an article for ''Slate'' arguing that there was "no such thing as a good Trump voter". Several days earlier, he compared Trump voters to the "angry, recalcitrant whites" who resisted the [[Reconstruction era]] after the [[American Civil War]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mediaite.com/online/slates-jamelle-bouie-says-there-are-no-good-trump-voters-compares-them-to-lynch-mobs/ |title=Slate's Jamelle Bouie Says There Are No Good Trump Voters, Compares Them To Lynch Mobs |last=Baragona |first=Justin |website=Mediaite |date=November 15, 2016 |access-date=April 4, 2017 |archive-date=November 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113215242/https://www.mediaite.com/online/slates-jamelle-bouie-says-there-are-no-good-trump-voters-compares-them-to-lynch-mobs/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2016/11/jamelle-bouie-trump-2016-presidential-election-231043 |title=Slate writer: Pro-Trump white 'backlash' evokes end of Reconstruction |last=Wermund |first=Benjamin |date=November 9, 2016 |work=Politico |access-date=April 4, 2017 |archive-date=April 30, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170430115525/http://www.politico.com/story/2016/11/jamelle-bouie-trump-2016-presidential-election-231043 |url-status=live }}</ref> He has criticized the media for an unwillingness to label [[racism in the United States]] as "racist", opting instead for terms such as "racial" and "racially charged".<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.cjr.org/covering_trump/trump-racism.php|last=Vernon|first=Pete|date=September 25, 2017|title=Dancing around the word 'racist' in coverage of Trump|magazine=Columbia Journalism Review|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=January 29, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190129142431/https://www.cjr.org/covering_trump/trump-racism.php|url-status=live}}</ref> He also criticized the media for its "horse-race" coverage of the 2016 presidential election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.poynter.org/reporting-editing/2016/heres-some-of-the-best-political-journalism-of-2016/|title=Here's (some of) the best political journalism of 2016|date=November 7, 2016|publisher=The Poynter Institute for Media Studies|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=October 31, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031215348/https://www.poynter.org/reporting-editing/2016/heres-some-of-the-best-political-journalism-of-2016/|url-status=live}}</ref> He called the [[NPR]] interview between [[Noel King (broadcast journalist)|Noel King]] and [[Jason Kessler]] "journalistic malpractice".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://money.cnn.com/2018/08/11/media/hate-group-gatherings-news-coverage-ethics/index.html|title=Stelter: How to report on racists without fanning the flames|last=Stelter|first=Brian|date=August 11, 2018|website=CNNMoney|access-date=January 25, 2019|archive-date=October 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181003102439/https://money.cnn.com/2018/08/11/media/hate-group-gatherings-news-coverage-ethics/index.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In 2019, ''The New York Times'' announced that Bouie would join its lineup of opinion columnists.<ref>{{cite press release |url=https://www.nytco.com/press/jamelle-bouie-joins-the-new-york-times-opinion-pages-as-columnist/ |title=Jamelle Bouie Joins The New York Times Opinion Pages as Columnist |publisher=The New York Times Company |date=January 3, 2019 |access-date=April 2, 2019 |archive-date=April 4, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190404044754/https://www.nytco.com/press/jamelle-bouie-joins-the-new-york-times-opinion-pages-as-columnist/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Bouie is an accomplished photographer.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Weber |first=Greta |title=Slate's Jamelle Bouie Is Also a Pretty Good Street Photographer - Washingtonian |url=https://washingtonian.com/2017/02/10/slates-jamelle-bouie-also-pretty-good-street-photographer/ |magazine=The Washingtonian |date=February 10, 2017}}</ref> His first public photography exhibition, in 2019, focused on towns in Oklahoma founded in the 19th century by former slaves.<ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Hare |first1=Erin |title=View finder: New York Times opinion writer Jamelle Bouie develops his perspective as a photographer |url=https://www.c-ville.com/view-finder-new-york-times-opinion-writer-jamelle-bouie-develops-his-perspective-as-a-photographer/ |work=C-VILLE Weekly |date=May 29, 2019}}</ref> With John Ganz, he hosts the ''Unclear and Present Danger'' podcast exploring political thriller films of the 1990s and what they say about the United States' perception of the world in that era.<ref name="Bouie 2023 c165">{{cite web | last=Bouie | first=Jamelle | title=Unclear and Present Danger | website=Jamelle Bouie | date=September 17, 2023 | url=https://jamellebouie.net/unclear-and-present-danger | access-date=September 22, 2023}}</ref> He also has an active presence on TikTok, which ''Slate'' noted as unusual for an opinion journalist working in [[legacy media]].<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Winkle |first=Luke|title=Jamelle Bouie Has It|magazine=Slate|date=December 16, 2024|url=https://slate.com/life/2024/12/tiktok-videos-new-york-times-politics-jamelle-bouie.html|access-date=January 17, 2025}}</ref>

== Recognition ==
In 2012, Bouie was chosen for ''[[The Root (magazine)|The Root]]''{{'}}s Root Top 100. The site wrote, "his is a strong, influential and necessary voice during the 2012 election season and beyond".<ref>{{cite magazine|title = The Root 100|url = http://www.theroot.com/articles/lists/2012/10/theroot_100/jamelle_bouie.html|magazine= The Root|date = October 1, 2012|access-date = January 18, 2016|archive-date = January 22, 2016|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160122023354/http://www.theroot.com/articles/lists/2012/10/theroot_100/jamelle_bouie.html|url-status = live}}</ref> ''[[Forbes (magazine)|Forbes]]'' recognized Bouie in its "30 Under 30 – Media" list in 2015, writing, "he became a leading voice on the Ferguson story".<ref>{{cite magazine|title = Jamelle Bouie, 27 - In Photos: 2015 30 Under 30: Media|url = https://www.forbes.com/pictures/flji45eefm/jamelle-bouie-27/|magazine= Forbes|access-date = January 18, 2016|archive-date = January 19, 2016|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160119190434/http://www.forbes.com/pictures/flji45eefm/jamelle-bouie-27/|url-status = live}}</ref> He received the [[American Political Science Association]]'s 2025 [[Carey McWilliams (journalist)|Carey McWilliams]] Award, which honors "a major journalistic contribution to our understanding of politics".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://politicalsciencenow.com/jamelle-bouie-receives-the-2025-carey-mcwilliams-award/|title=Jamelle Bouie Receives the 2025 Carey McWilliams Award -|first=Lilly|last=Guthrie|date=August 25, 2025}}</ref> In 2019, in the ''[[Columbia Journalism Review]]'', David Uberti called Bouie "one of the defining commentators on politics and race in the Trump era."<ref>{{cite magazine| url= https://www.cjr.org/analysis/slate-magazine-podcast-trump-staff-departures.php| title= The future of the #SlatePitch in Trump's world| last= Uberti| first= David| magazine= Columbia Journalism Review| date= January 16, 2019| access-date= January 25, 2019| archive-date= January 16, 2019| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190116192226/https://www.cjr.org/analysis/slate-magazine-podcast-trump-staff-departures.php| url-status= live}}</ref>

