'''Béatrice Lamarche''' ({{IPA|fr|beatʁis lamaʁʃ}}; born 1 October 1998) is a Canadian [[Long track speed skating|long track speed skater]], who has been active since 2010.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.speedskatingstats.com/index.php?file=skater&code=1998100101|title= SpeedSkatingStats.com|website=speedskatingstats.com|accessdate=3 April 2020}}</ref> Lamarche is a sprinter.

As a junior, Lamarche became national champion five times and participated at the 2016, 2017 and 2018 [[World Junior Speed Skating Championships]] and [[ISU Junior World Cup Speed Skating]] events. At the elite level she made her [[ISU Speed Skating World Cup]] debut during the 2016-17 speed skating season.<ref name=speedskatingnews /> She won at the 2019 [[Canadian Single Distance Championships]] the bronze medal in the 1000 metres event.<ref name=speedskatingnews /> Due to that result, she represented her nation at the [[2020 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships]] in the [[2020 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships – Women's 1000 metres|1000 metres event]], finishing 21st.<ref name=speedskatingnews>{{Cite web|url=https://www.speedskatingnews.info/en/data/skater/beatrice-lamarche/|title=Competition results, statistics and records; SpeedSkatingNews|website=www.speedskatingnews.info|accessdate=3 April 2020}}</ref>

She is the daughter of former olympian speed skater [[Ben Lamarche]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.isu.html.infostradasports.com/cache/TheASP.asp%40PageID%3D103037%26SportID%3D103%26Personid%3D1470283%26TaalCode%3D2%26StyleID%3D0%26Cache%3D2.html|title=Biographies / Canada / Béatrice Lamarche|quote=Famous relatives: Her father Benoit Lamarche represented Canada in speed skating at the Olympic Winter Games in 1984 and 1988. (SportsDeskOnline, 24 Nov 2016; speedskating.ca, 22 Oct 2016)|website=www.isu.html.infostradasports.com|accessdate=22 February 2021|archive-date=2 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210502201606/http://www.isu.html.infostradasports.com/cache/TheASP.asp%40PageID%3D103037%26SportID%3D103%26Personid%3D1470283%26TaalCode%3D2%26StyleID%3D0%26Cache%3D2.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> and a niece of former olympian speed skater [[Marie-Pierre Lamarche]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.olympedia.org/athletes/81615|title=Home / Athletes / Marie-Pierre Lamarche|quote=Olympic family relations: Sister of Ben Lamarche|website=www.olympedia.org|accessdate=22 February 2021}}</ref>

